import Becoming.Response
import Becoming.Mechanism
import Becoming.Unfolding

/-!
# Counterfactual: the course the dynamics give

`Becoming.Response` (P65) decided the comparison where the rest responds within a part's
responsive reach, with two things supplied: the second flow, and the premise that the rest
responds through its registration (`Flow.RespondsThroughCoupling`). The chapter's open item
asked for "a comparison in which others respond as the dynamics give, not through a second flow
supplied". This module gives the rest's exchange by a law and derives both. No premise enters
beyond the law of exchange itself and what each theorem names.

* **A law of exchange.** What each formation takes from and gives to each condition across a step
  is read off a level history (`ExchangeLaw`). The law reads what has stood when, across the step
  from `t`, it reads the history up to `t` only (`ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating`). A flow follows
  the law outside a part when every formation outside the part exchanges as the law gives under
  the flow's own levels (`Flow.Follows`).
* **The counterfactual is fixed by the act.** Two balanced flows with the same formations present,
  in which the part acts alike (`Flow.ActsAlike`) and the rest follows one law of what has stood,
  starting alike, have the same levels at every step and the same exchange
  (`levels_fixed`, `counterfactual_fixed`). So once the law is given, the course of an act is not
  supplied: at most one course is it.
* **It exists exactly where it is feasible.** The law generates a history step by step from
  starting levels (`ExchangeLaw.history`, `ExchangeLaw.levels`, `ExchangeLaw.course`), the
  orbit of one determined law on the stage and the history so far (`ExchangeLaw.history_orbit`).
  Under a law of what has stood, the generated flow follows the law (`ExchangeLaw.course_follows`)
  and balances exactly where the law never takes more from a condition than stands on it and is
  given to it (`ExchangeLaw.Feasible`, `ExchangeLaw.course_balances_iff`). The part acting by
  another law of what has stood (`ExchangeLaw.withAct`, `ExchangeLaw.withAct_nonanticipating`), a
  balanced flow in which the part acts as in that course and the rest follows the law, starting at
  the course's levels, has the course's levels and exchange (`counterfactual_is_course`): the
  counterfactual of the act is the course it generates. Where every formation follows one law of
  what has stood, the part included, a balanced flow is the course the law generates
  (`ExchangeLaw.course_unique`).
* **The response premise follows.** Where the law reads, for each formation outside the part,
  only what that formation registers (`ExchangeLaw.Through`), the rest responds through its
  registration in any two courses of the law (`ExchangeLaw.Through.responds`): P65's premise, with
  nothing supplied about the pair. Every law of what has stood reads through registering
  everything (`ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating.through_all`). With finitely many conditions it reads
  through registering what its exchange is sensitive to (`ExchangeLaw.Sensitive`,
  `ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating.through_sensitive`), and through every registration that observes
  that (`ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating.through_of_observed`), the proof changing one condition at a
  time (`alike_of_sensitive_agree`). Conversely every registration a law reads through holds,
  with what the formation depends on, every condition its exchange is sensitive to, up to double
  negation: that it leaves one out is refuted (`¬ ¬`, `ExchangeLaw.Through.couples_of_sensitive`).
  So sensitivity is the least registration, least in that double-negation form. No
  decision of sensitivity is used: what is decided is only whether two numbers are equal
  (`nat_eq_of_not_not`).
* **The comparison as the dynamics give.** Two balanced courses of one law reading through a
  registration, with the same formations present and starting alike: for any significance
  covering what is not beyond the part's responsive reach drawn through that registration, each
  retains along the other exactly as it includes it, and neither adds exactly where neither
  exceeds (`comparison_of_courses`, from `Flow.Balances.comparison_within_response`). With finitely many
  conditions the registration need not be given: it is the law's sensitivity
  (`comparison_through_sensitivity`). With the other course generated by another act of the part,
  feasible, the comparison of an act with an act not taken holds with neither the second flow nor
  the response supplied (`comparison_with_act`).
* **The act is the input of a standard causal model.** The history generated where the part makes
  a sequence of moves (`ExchangeLaw.ofMoves`) is the trajectory of the standard model of
  `Becoming.Mechanism` (P68) whose input is the part's move (`ExchangeLaw.stepBy`,
  `ExchangeLaw.course_trajectory`). An act not taken is a move not taken: the record of a run of
  that model fixes no outcome of it, while the model fixes it (`ExchangeLaw.act_not_taken`, from
  `inputs_intervention_unfixed` and `inputs_model_fixes`).
* **The counterfactual as an unfolding (P80b, with `Becoming.Unfolding`).** Over one kind of
  relatum the course is a world (`ExchangeLaw.world`), which balances, and so runs by the law of
  exchange, exactly where the course is feasible (`ExchangeLaw.world_balances_iff`,
  `ExchangeLaw.world_exchange_iff`, `ExchangeLaw.worldRun`): the course of an act not taken has
  the form of every unfolding, not a second kind of thing. A determined law steps beyond what a
  history of it shows exactly at the moves the history never takes there (`Run.beyond_shown_iff`,
  a law about P69's `Shown`). So the history an act generates
  shows a determined law, and an act has a step of the standard model beyond it exactly where the
  history never takes that act there (`ExchangeLaw.counterfactual_beyond_shown`): what the law
  adds to the history is exactly its counterfactuals. With the part's act formed by a law on the
  configuration (`ExchangeLaw.policy`), a run of P69's `closedBy` from the starting levels is the
  course that act generates (`ExchangeLaw.closedBy_course`), so two such runs agree
  (`closedBy_runs_agree`).
* **Witness.** In P65's `Loop` the second's reply is a law (`Loop.replyLaw`) that reads what has
  stood and only what the second observes (`Loop.replyLaw_nonanticipating`,
  `Loop.replyLaw_through`). The flow with the first's act and the flow without both follow it
  (`Loop.escalation_follows`, `Loop.quiet_follows`), so P65's `Loop.responds`, checked there case
  by case, follows from the law (`Loop.responds_by_law`); and each is the only balanced course of
  the law with its act (`Loop.courses_fixed`).

**Readings (the AI's, adopted on Ren's delegation of 15 October 2026, DELEGATED-CHOICES D25).**
- A counterfactual is not a second world beside the actual one. It is the course the same law
  gives with the part acting otherwise, fixed by the act where it exists, and it exists only where
  the law could be followed: a course that would take more than stands and is given is no
  course.
- For a rest following a law of what has stood, P65's response premise is no further assumption
  about how formations behave. Whatever such law their exchange follows, it reads through a
  registration, with finitely many conditions what the exchange is sensitive to; read as 识, a
  formation registers what its own exchange is sensitive to. The narrower that
  sensitivity, the narrower the reach in which the difference an act makes can arrive.
- An act not taken is an intervention in the sense of the standard causal model: the record does
  not fix its outcome, and the law does.
- With P69 (Ren: "there are no two realities"): the course of an act not taken has the form of
  every unfolding, and what a law adds to the history it governs is exactly the moves the
  history never takes, so a counterfactual is a law's step beyond its history, not a second
  history beside it. With the
  part's act formed within the unfolding, the course is fixed from the start: an act otherwise is
  a law of the part otherwise, what one does otherwise being what one is otherwise.

What is not derived: the law of exchange itself (which law the rest follows); that it reads what
has stood (`Nonanticipating`, a hypothesis wherever it enters); feasibility of a course (a
hypothesis of `comparison_with_act`, `counterfactual_is_course` and `ExchangeLaw.course_unique`,
proved equivalent to the generated flow's balance);
that the least registration is sensitivity when there are infinitely many conditions (only
finitely many are treated, listed, with equality decidable); any measure of how much difference
arrives; when a move is the part's own rather than supplied (`ExchangeLaw.closedBy_course` takes
the act as formed by a law); a law over two kinds of relatum, formations and conditions, read as
one world. The representation commits to discrete steps and natural amounts. No ethical predicate,
ranking or axiom occurs.
-/

namespace Becoming

open Generative

variable {F C : Type}

/-- A law of exchange: what each formation takes from and gives to each condition across the step
from `t`, read off a level history. -/
structure ExchangeLaw (F C : Type) where
  take : Nat → F → (Nat → C → Nat) → C → Nat
  give : Nat → F → (Nat → C → Nat) → C → Nat

/-- Two level histories agree at every step up to `t`. -/
def AgreeUpTo (l l' : Nat → C → Nat) (t : Nat) : Prop :=
  ∀ s, s ≤ t → ∀ c, l s c = l' s c

namespace ExchangeLaw

variable (law : ExchangeLaw F C)

/-- The law gives alike under two histories at `t` for `h`. -/
def GivesAlike (t : Nat) (h : F) (l l' : Nat → C → Nat) : Prop :=
  ∀ c, law.take t h l c = law.take t h l' c ∧ law.give t h l c = law.give t h l' c

/-- The law reads what has stood: across the step from `t` it reads the history up to `t` only. -/
def Nonanticipating : Prop :=
  ∀ t h l l', AgreeUpTo l l' t → law.GivesAlike t h l l'

/-- The law reads, for each formation outside the part, only what that formation registers. -/
def Through (W : Web F C) (obs : F → C → Prop) (part : F → Bool) : Prop :=
  ∀ h, part h = false → ∀ t l l', W.AlikeThroughUpTo obs h l l' t → law.GivesAlike t h l l'

/-- Across the step from `t`, what `h` exchanges is fixed by the other conditions' levels up to
`t`: `c`'s levels, and every level after `t`, make no difference to it. -/
def InsensitiveAt (t : Nat) (h : F) (c : C) : Prop :=
  ∀ l l', (∀ s, s ≤ t → ∀ e, e ≠ c → l s e = l' s e) → law.GivesAlike t h l l'

/-- `h`'s exchange is sensitive to `c`: at some step changing `c`'s levels alone changes it. -/
def Sensitive (h : F) (c : C) : Prop :=
  ∃ t, ¬ law.InsensitiveAt t h c

end ExchangeLaw

namespace Flow

/-- Outside the part, the flow exchanges as the law gives under the level history `l`. -/
def Follows (L : Flow F C) (law : ExchangeLaw F C) (part : F → Bool) (l : Nat → C → Nat) :
    Prop :=
  ∀ h, part h = false → ∀ t c, L.take t h c = law.take t h l c ∧ L.give t h c = law.give t h l c

/-- The part acts alike in two flows. -/
def ActsAlike (L L' : Flow F C) (part : F → Bool) : Prop :=
  ∀ h, part h = true → ∀ t c, L.take t h c = L'.take t h c ∧ L.give t h c = L'.give t h c

end Flow

/-! ## Responding through registration, derived -/

section Responds

variable {law : ExchangeLaw F C} {L L' : Flow F C} {W W' : Web F C} {obs : F → C → Prop}
  {part : F → Bool}

/-- **The response premise follows from the law.** Where the rest follows one law that reads,
for each formation, only what it registers, the rest responds through its registration in any
two courses: P65's premise, with no premise on the pair. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.Through.responds (through : law.Through W obs part)
    (follows : L.Follows law part W.level) (follows' : L'.Follows law part W'.level) :
    L.RespondsThroughCoupling L' W W' obs part :=
  fun h out t alike c =>
    have same := through h out t W.level W'.level alike c
    ⟨(follows h out t c).1.trans (same.1.trans (follows' h out t c).1.symm),
     (follows h out t c).2.trans (same.2.trans (follows' h out t c).2.symm)⟩

/-- Every law of what has stood reads through registering everything. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating.through_all (na : law.Nonanticipating) (W : Web F C)
    (part : F → Bool) : law.Through W (fun _ _ => True) part :=
  fun h _ t l l' alike => na t h l l' fun s le e => (alike s le).2 e trivial

end Responds

/-! ## The counterfactual is fixed by the act -/

section Unique

variable {law : ExchangeLaw F C} {L L' : Flow F C} {W W' : Web F C} {part : F → Bool}

/-- Where everyone exchanges alike across the step from `t`, the totals are alike. -/
theorem Flow.totals_alike_of_each (present : L.present t = L'.present t)
    (each : ∀ h c, L.take t h c = L'.take t h c ∧ L.give t h c = L'.give t h c) (c : C) :
    L.taken t c = L'.taken t c ∧ L.given t c = L'.given t c :=
  ⟨(total_congr_on fun h _ => (each h c).1).trans
     (congrArg (fun as => total as fun h => L'.take t h c) present),
   (total_congr_on fun h _ => (each h c).2).trans
     (congrArg (fun as => total as fun h => L'.give t h c) present)⟩

/-- Where the levels agree up to `t`, the part acts alike and the rest follows one law of what
has stood, everyone exchanges alike across the step from `t`. -/
theorem exchange_alike_of_agree (na : law.Nonanticipating) (acts : L.ActsAlike L' part)
    (follows : L.Follows law part W.level) (follows' : L'.Follows law part W'.level) {t : Nat}
    (agree : AgreeUpTo W.level W'.level t) :
    ∀ h c, L.take t h c = L'.take t h c ∧ L.give t h c = L'.give t h c :=
  fun h c =>
    match hp : part h with
    | true => acts h hp t c
    | false =>
      have same := na t h W.level W'.level agree c
      ⟨(follows h hp t c).1.trans (same.1.trans (follows' h hp t c).1.symm),
       (follows h hp t c).2.trans (same.2.trans (follows' h hp t c).2.symm)⟩

/-- **The levels of the counterfactual are fixed by the act.** Two balanced flows with the same
formations present, in which the part acts alike and the rest follows one law of what has stood,
starting alike, have the same levels at every step. -/
theorem levels_fixed (na : law.Nonanticipating) (balances : L.Balances W)
    (balances' : L'.Balances W') (present : ∀ t, L.present t = L'.present t)
    (acts : L.ActsAlike L' part) (follows : L.Follows law part W.level)
    (follows' : L'.Follows law part W'.level) (start : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = W'.level 0 c) :
    ∀ t, AgreeUpTo W.level W'.level t
  | 0 => fun s le c =>
    match s, le with
    | 0, _ => start c
  | t + 1 => fun s le c =>
    have agree := levels_fixed na balances balances' present acts follows follows' start t
    match le_succ_cases le with
    | .inl before => agree s before c
    | .inr now =>
      have totals := Flow.totals_alike_of_each (present t)
        (exchange_alike_of_agree na acts follows follows' agree) c
      now ▸ add_right_cancel (L.taken t c)
        ((balances t c).trans
          ((congrArg (· + L.given t c) (agree t (Nat.le_refl t) c)).trans
            ((congrArg (W'.level t c + ·) totals.2).trans
              ((balances' t c).symm.trans (congrArg (W'.level (t + 1) c + ·) totals.1.symm)))))

/-- **The counterfactual is fixed by the act.** Under the same hypotheses the two flows are one
flow: every formation takes and gives alike at every step. -/
theorem counterfactual_fixed (na : law.Nonanticipating) (balances : L.Balances W)
    (balances' : L'.Balances W') (present : ∀ t, L.present t = L'.present t)
    (acts : L.ActsAlike L' part) (follows : L.Follows law part W.level)
    (follows' : L'.Follows law part W'.level) (start : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = W'.level 0 c) :
    (∀ t c, W.level t c = W'.level t c) ∧
      ∀ t h c, L.take t h c = L'.take t h c ∧ L.give t h c = L'.give t h c :=
  ⟨fun t c => levels_fixed na balances balances' present acts follows follows' start t t
      (Nat.le_refl t) c,
   fun t => exchange_alike_of_agree na acts follows follows'
      (levels_fixed na balances balances' present acts follows follows' start t)⟩

end Unique

/-! ## The counterfactual, generated -/

namespace ExchangeLaw

variable (law : ExchangeLaw F C) (present : Nat → List F)

/-- What the law takes from `c` in total across the step from `n` under the history `l`. -/
def takenUnder (l : Nat → C → Nat) (n : Nat) (c : C) : Nat :=
  total (present n) fun f => law.take n f l c

/-- What the law gives to `c` in total across the step from `n` under the history `l`. -/
def givenUnder (l : Nat → C → Nat) (n : Nat) (c : C) : Nat :=
  total (present n) fun f => law.give n f l c

/-- The history `l` carried across the step from `n`: as it is up to `n`, and after `n` the level
the law gives across that step. -/
def extend (l : Nat → C → Nat) (n : Nat) : Nat → C → Nat :=
  fun s c => if s ≤ n then l s c else l n c + law.givenUnder present l n c - law.takenUnder present l n c

/-- The history the law generates up to stage `n` from the starting levels. -/
def history (start : C → Nat) : Nat → Nat → C → Nat
  | 0 => fun _ => start
  | n + 1 => law.extend present (history start n) n

/-- The levels the law generates. -/
def levels (start : C → Nat) : Nat → C → Nat :=
  fun t => law.history present start t t

/-- The flow the law generates: the formations present exchange as the law gives under the levels
it generates. -/
def course (start : C → Nat) : Flow F C :=
  ⟨present, fun t f c => law.take t f (law.levels present start) c,
    fun t f c => law.give t f (law.levels present start) c⟩

/-- The law never takes more from a condition than stands on it and is given to it. -/
def Feasible (start : C → Nat) : Prop :=
  ∀ t c, (law.course present start).taken t c ≤
    law.levels present start t c + (law.course present start).given t c

/-- The part acts by `act` and the rest by `law`. -/
def withAct (part : F → Bool) (act : ExchangeLaw F C) : ExchangeLaw F C :=
  ⟨fun t h l c => bif part h then act.take t h l c else law.take t h l c,
    fun t h l c => bif part h then act.give t h l c else law.give t h l c⟩

/-- One step of generation, the configuration being the stage and the history so far. -/
def next : Nat × (Nat → C → Nat) → Nat × (Nat → C → Nat) :=
  fun x => (x.1 + 1, law.extend present x.2 x.1)

end ExchangeLaw

/-- `W` read with the levels `l`. -/
def Web.withLevel (W : Web F C) (l : Nat → C → Nat) : Web F C :=
  ⟨l, W.active, W.requires, W.need, W.draws⟩

section Generation

variable {law : ExchangeLaw F C} {present : Nat → List F} {start : C → Nat}

namespace ExchangeLaw

theorem history_succ_of_le {n s : Nat} (le : s ≤ n) (c : C) :
    law.history present start (n + 1) s c = law.history present start n s c :=
  if_pos le

/-- Generating further keeps what has been generated. -/
theorem history_stable {n s : Nat} (le : s ≤ n) (c : C) :
    ∀ d, law.history present start (n + d) s c = law.history present start n s c
  | 0 => rfl
  | d + 1 => (history_succ_of_le (Nat.le_trans le (Nat.le_add_right n d)) c).trans
      (history_stable le c d)

/-- The history generated to stage `n` is the generated levels up to `n`. -/
theorem history_agree (n : Nat) : AgreeUpTo (law.history present start n) (law.levels present start) n :=
  fun s le c =>
    let ⟨d, gap⟩ := gap_of_le le
    (congrArg (fun m => law.history present start m s c) gap).trans
      (history_stable (Nat.le_refl s) c d)

/-- The generated levels start at the starting levels. -/
theorem levels_zero (c : C) : law.levels present start 0 c = start c := rfl

/-- Across the step from `n`, the generated level is the level before, with what the law gives,
less what it takes, under the history so far. -/
theorem levels_succ (n : Nat) (c : C) :
    law.levels present start (n + 1) c =
      law.levels present start n c + law.givenUnder present (law.history present start n) n c -
        law.takenUnder present (law.history present start n) n c :=
  if_neg (Nat.not_succ_le_self n)

/-- The generated flow follows the law, for every formation and so for every part. -/
theorem course_follows (part : F → Bool) :
    (law.course present start).Follows law part (law.levels present start) :=
  fun _ _ _ _ => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩

/-- Under a law of what has stood, the totals under the history so far are the generated flow's. -/
theorem totals_under (na : law.Nonanticipating) (n : Nat) (c : C) :
    law.takenUnder present (law.history present start n) n c = (law.course present start).taken n c ∧
      law.givenUnder present (law.history present start n) n c =
        (law.course present start).given n c :=
  ⟨total_congr_on fun f _ => (na n f _ _ (history_agree n) c).1,
   total_congr_on fun f _ => (na n f _ _ (history_agree n) c).2⟩

/-- What is taken away, added back, restores what was there, where no more is taken than is
there. -/
theorem sub_add_back : ∀ {m k : Nat}, k ≤ m → m - k + k = m
  | _, 0, _ => rfl
  | 0, k + 1, le => absurd le (Nat.not_succ_le_zero k)
  | m + 1, k + 1, le =>
    (congrArg (· + (k + 1)) (Nat.succ_sub_succ m k)).trans
      (congrArg Nat.succ (sub_add_back (Nat.le_of_succ_le_succ le)))

/-- `m - k` with `k` added back is `m` exactly when `k ≤ m`. -/
theorem sub_add_back_iff {m k : Nat} : m - k + k = m ↔ k ≤ m :=
  ⟨fun back => Nat.le_trans (Nat.le_add_left k (m - k)) (Nat.le_of_eq back), sub_add_back⟩

/-- **The counterfactual exists where it is feasible.** Under a law of what has stood, the flow
the law generates balances on the levels it generates exactly where the law never takes more
from a condition than stands on it and is given to it. -/
theorem course_balances_iff (na : law.Nonanticipating) (W : Web F C) :
    (law.course present start).Balances (W.withLevel (law.levels present start)) ↔
      law.Feasible present start :=
  have step : ∀ t c, law.levels present start (t + 1) c =
      law.levels present start t c + (law.course present start).given t c -
        (law.course present start).taken t c := fun t c =>
    have totals := totals_under (start := start) na t c
    (levels_succ t c).trans
      ((congrArg (fun g => law.levels present start t c + g -
          law.takenUnder present (law.history present start t) t c) totals.2).trans
        (congrArg (fun k => law.levels present start t c + (law.course present start).given t c - k)
          totals.1))
  ⟨fun balances t c =>
    sub_add_back_iff.mp ((congrArg (· + (law.course present start).taken t c) (step t c)).symm.trans
      (balances t c)),
   fun feasible t c =>
    (congrArg (· + (law.course present start).taken t c) (step t c)).trans
      (sub_add_back_iff.mpr (feasible t c))⟩

/-- The generated history is the orbit of one determined law on the stage and the history so
far. -/
theorem history_orbit (start : C → Nat) :
    ∀ n, Law.orbit (law.next present) (0, fun _ => start) n = (n, law.history present start n)
  | 0 => rfl
  | n + 1 => congrArg (law.next present) (history_orbit start n)

end ExchangeLaw

end Generation

/-! ## The registration a law reads through -/

section Sensitivity

variable {law : ExchangeLaw F C}

/-- An equality of numbers that is not refuted holds. -/
theorem nat_eq_of_not_not {a b : Nat} (nn : ¬ ¬ a = b) : a = b :=
  match Nat.decEq a b with
  | isTrue same => same
  | isFalse differ => absurd differ nn

/-- **A law reading through a registration is sensitive only to what that registration holds.**
Where a law reads, for a formation outside the part, only what it registers, every condition its
exchange is sensitive to is one the formation depends on or observes: it is not left out. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.Through.couples_of_sensitive {W : Web F C} {obs : F → C → Prop}
    {part : F → Bool} (through : law.Through W obs part) {h : F} (out : part h = false) {c : C}
    (sensitive : law.Sensitive h c) : ¬ ¬ W.CoupledInto obs h c :=
  fun uncoupled =>
    let ⟨t, notInsensitive⟩ := sensitive
    notInsensitive fun l l' off =>
      have apart : ∀ e, W.CoupledInto obs h e → e ≠ c := fun e registers same => by
        subst same
        exact uncoupled registers
      through h out t l l' fun s le =>
        ⟨⟨fun admits e requires =>
            (off s le e (apart e (Or.inl ⟨s, requires⟩))) ▸ admits e requires,
          fun admits e requires =>
            (off s le e (apart e (Or.inl ⟨s, requires⟩))).symm ▸ admits e requires⟩,
         fun e seen => off s le e (apart e (Or.inr seen))⟩

variable [DecidableEq C]

/-- `l` with `l'` mixed in on the conditions of a list. -/
def mixIn (l l' : Nat → C → Nat) : List C → Nat → C → Nat
  | [] => l
  | c :: ds => fun s e => if e = c then l' s e else mixIn l l' ds s e

theorem mixIn_either (l l' : Nat → C → Nat) :
    ∀ (ds : List C) (s : Nat) (e : C), mixIn l l' ds s e = l s e ∨ mixIn l l' ds s e = l' s e
  | [], _, _ => .inl rfl
  | c :: ds, s, e =>
    match (inferInstance : Decidable (e = c)) with
    | isTrue same => .inr (if_pos same)
    | isFalse differ =>
      have back : (if e = c then l' s e else mixIn l l' ds s e) = mixIn l l' ds s e :=
        if_neg differ
      match mixIn_either l l' ds s e with
      | .inl one => .inl (back.trans one)
      | .inr other => .inr (back.trans other)

theorem mixIn_listed (l l' : Nat → C → Nat) :
    ∀ {ds : List C} {e : C}, e ∈ ds → ∀ s, mixIn l l' ds s e = l' s e
  | c :: ds, e, mem, s =>
    match (inferInstance : Decidable (e = c)) with
    | isTrue same => if_pos same
    | isFalse differ =>
      (if_neg differ : (if e = c then l' s e else mixIn l l' ds s e) = mixIn l l' ds s e).trans
        (mixIn_listed l l'
          (match mem_cons_split mem with
            | .inl same => absurd same differ
            | .inr rest => rest) s)

/-- Changing one condition's levels changes nothing a law of what has stood gives, where the law
is not sensitive to it or the two histories agree on it. -/
theorem swap_alike (na : law.Nonanticipating) {t : Nat} {h : F} {c : C}
    {X l l' : Nat → C → Nat} (either : ∀ s, s ≤ t → X s c = l s c ∨ X s c = l' s c)
    (agree : law.Sensitive h c → ∀ s, s ≤ t → l s c = l' s c) :
    law.GivesAlike t h X (mixIn X l' [c]) :=
  have off : ∀ s, s ≤ t → ∀ e, e ≠ c → X s e = mixIn X l' [c] s e :=
    fun _ _ _ differ => (if_neg differ).symm
  have whenSensitive : law.Sensitive h c →
      law.GivesAlike t h X (mixIn X l' [c]) := fun sensitive =>
    na t h _ _ fun s le e =>
      match (inferInstance : Decidable (e = c)) with
      | isTrue same => by
        subst same
        exact (match either s le with
          | .inl one => one.trans (agree sensitive s le)
          | .inr other => other).trans (if_pos rfl).symm
      | isFalse differ => (if_neg differ).symm
  fun d =>
    ⟨nat_eq_of_not_not fun differ =>
      differ ((whenSensitive ⟨t, fun insensitive => differ ((insensitive _ _ off d).1)⟩) d).1,
     nat_eq_of_not_not fun differ =>
      differ ((whenSensitive ⟨t, fun insensitive => differ ((insensitive _ _ off d).2)⟩) d).2⟩

theorem mixIn_alike (na : law.Nonanticipating) {t : Nat} {h : F} {l l' : Nat → C → Nat}
    (agree : ∀ c, law.Sensitive h c → ∀ s, s ≤ t → l s c = l' s c) :
    ∀ ds, law.GivesAlike t h l (mixIn l l' ds)
  | [] => fun _ => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | c :: ds => fun d =>
    have before := mixIn_alike na agree ds d
    have across := swap_alike (X := mixIn l l' ds) na
      (fun s _ => mixIn_either l l' ds s c) (agree c) d
    ⟨before.1.trans across.1, before.2.trans across.2⟩

/-- **A law of what has stood reads through what it is sensitive to.** With finitely many
conditions, it gives alike under two histories that agree, up to the step, on every condition it
is sensitive to. -/
theorem alike_of_sensitive_agree (na : law.Nonanticipating) {cs : List C}
    (every : ∀ c, c ∈ cs) {t : Nat} {h : F} {l l' : Nat → C → Nat}
    (agree : ∀ c, law.Sensitive h c → ∀ s, s ≤ t → l s c = l' s c) : law.GivesAlike t h l l' :=
  fun d =>
    have chain := mixIn_alike na agree cs d
    have last := na t h (mixIn l l' cs) l' (fun s _ c => mixIn_listed l l' (every c) s) d
    ⟨chain.1.trans last.1, chain.2.trans last.2⟩

/-- So, with finitely many conditions, a law of what has stood reads through every registration
that observes what it is sensitive to. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating.through_of_observed (na : law.Nonanticipating)
    {cs : List C} (every : ∀ c, c ∈ cs) {W : Web F C} {obs : F → C → Prop} {part : F → Bool}
    (observed : ∀ h, part h = false → ∀ c, law.Sensitive h c → ¬ ¬ obs h c) :
    law.Through W obs part :=
  fun h out _ _ _ alike => alike_of_sensitive_agree na every fun c sensitive s le =>
    nat_eq_of_not_not fun differ =>
      observed h out c sensitive fun seen => differ ((alike s le).2 c seen)

/-- **Every law of what has stood reads through a registration: what it is sensitive to.** With
finitely many conditions, the law reads, for each formation, only the conditions its exchange is
sensitive to. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.Nonanticipating.through_sensitive (na : law.Nonanticipating) {cs : List C}
    (every : ∀ c, c ∈ cs) (W : Web F C) (part : F → Bool) : law.Through W law.Sensitive part :=
  na.through_of_observed every fun _ _ _ sensitive unseen => unseen sensitive

end Sensitivity

/-! ## The comparison as the dynamics give -/

section Comparison

variable {law act : ExchangeLaw F C} {L L' : Flow F C} {W W' : Web F C} {obs : F → C → Prop}
  {part : F → Bool}

/-- **The comparison where the rest responds, as the dynamics give.** Let two balanced flows with
the same formations present start alike, the rest following, in both, one law that reads through
a registration. For any significance that, from `p`, covers every formation not beyond the part's
responsive reach drawn through that registration, the levels of each flow retain what has
significance along the other's exactly as they include them, and neither adds a continuation with
significance exactly where neither exceeds the other. No response premise. -/
theorem comparison_of_courses {Pos : Type} (through : law.Through W obs part)
    (balances : L.Balances W) (balances' : L'.Balances W')
    (present : ∀ t, L.present t = L'.present t) (start : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = W'.level 0 c)
    (follows : L.Follows law part W.level) (follows' : L'.Follows law part W'.level)
    {sig : Pos → Nat → F → Prop} {p : Pos}
    (covers : ∀ t g, ¬ ¬ L.WithinResponse L' W obs part g → sig p t g) :
    (W.Retains sig p W.level W'.level ↔ W.Includes W.level W'.level) ∧
      (W.Retains sig p W'.level W.level ↔ W.Includes W'.level W.level) ∧
      (¬ W.Adds sig p W.level W'.level ↔ ¬ W.Exceeds W.level W'.level) ∧
      (¬ W.Adds sig p W'.level W.level ↔ ¬ W.Exceeds W'.level W.level) :=
  balances.comparison_within_response balances' present start (through.responds follows follows')
    covers

/-- With finitely many conditions the registration need not be given: the comparison of two
courses of a law of what has stood is decided within the responsive reach drawn through what the
law is sensitive to. -/
theorem comparison_through_sensitivity [DecidableEq C] {Pos : Type}
    (na : law.Nonanticipating) {cs : List C} (every : ∀ c, c ∈ cs)
    (balances : L.Balances W) (balances' : L'.Balances W')
    (present : ∀ t, L.present t = L'.present t) (start : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = W'.level 0 c)
    (follows : L.Follows law part W.level) (follows' : L'.Follows law part W'.level)
    {sig : Pos → Nat → F → Prop} {p : Pos}
    (covers : ∀ t g, ¬ ¬ L.WithinResponse L' W law.Sensitive part g → sig p t g) :
    (W.Retains sig p W.level W'.level ↔ W.Includes W.level W'.level) ∧
      (W.Retains sig p W'.level W.level ↔ W.Includes W'.level W.level) ∧
      (¬ W.Adds sig p W.level W'.level ↔ ¬ W.Exceeds W.level W'.level) ∧
      (¬ W.Adds sig p W'.level W.level ↔ ¬ W.Exceeds W'.level W.level) :=
  comparison_of_courses (na.through_sensitive every W part) balances balances' present start
    follows follows' covers

/-- The rest follows its law in the course where the part acts by `act`. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.withAct_follows (present : Nat → List F) (start : C → Nat) :
    ((law.withAct part act).course present start).Follows law part
      ((law.withAct part act).levels present start) :=
  fun _ out t c =>
    ⟨congrArg (fun b => bif b then act.take t _ ((law.withAct part act).levels present start) c
        else law.take t _ ((law.withAct part act).levels present start) c) out,
     congrArg (fun b => bif b then act.give t _ ((law.withAct part act).levels present start) c
        else law.give t _ ((law.withAct part act).levels present start) c) out⟩

/-- The part acting by a law of what has stood and the rest by another, together they form one. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.withAct_nonanticipating (na : law.Nonanticipating)
    (naAct : act.Nonanticipating) : (law.withAct part act).Nonanticipating :=
  fun t h l l' agree c => by
    show ((bif part h then act.take t h l c else law.take t h l c) =
        bif part h then act.take t h l' c else law.take t h l' c) ∧
      ((bif part h then act.give t h l c else law.give t h l c) =
        bif part h then act.give t h l' c else law.give t h l' c)
    cases part h
    · exact na t h l l' agree c
    · exact naAct t h l l' agree c

/-- **The comparison with an act not taken, as the dynamics give.** Let a balanced flow's rest
follow a law of what has stood reading through a registration, and let the part act instead by
`act`, a law of what has stood, where the course this generates is feasible. Then the comparison
of the flow with the course generated by the other act is decided within the part's responsive
reach drawn through the registration: the counterfactual is generated, not supplied, and no
response premise enters. -/
theorem comparison_with_act {Pos : Type} (na : law.Nonanticipating)
    (naAct : act.Nonanticipating) (through : law.Through W obs part) (balances : L.Balances W)
    (follows : L.Follows law part W.level)
    (feasible : (law.withAct part act).Feasible L.present (W.level 0))
    {sig : Pos → Nat → F → Prop} {p : Pos}
    (covers : ∀ t g, ¬ ¬ L.WithinResponse ((law.withAct part act).course L.present (W.level 0))
      W obs part g → sig p t g) :
    (W.Retains sig p W.level ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)) ↔
        W.Includes W.level ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0))) ∧
      (W.Retains sig p ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)) W.level ↔
        W.Includes ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)) W.level) ∧
      (¬ W.Adds sig p W.level ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)) ↔
        ¬ W.Exceeds W.level ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0))) ∧
      (¬ W.Adds sig p ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)) W.level ↔
        ¬ W.Exceeds ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)) W.level) :=
  comparison_of_courses (L' := (law.withAct part act).course L.present (W.level 0))
    (W' := W.withLevel ((law.withAct part act).levels L.present (W.level 0)))
    through balances
    ((ExchangeLaw.course_balances_iff (ExchangeLaw.withAct_nonanticipating na naAct) W).mpr feasible)
    (fun _ => rfl) (fun _ => rfl) follows (ExchangeLaw.withAct_follows L.present (W.level 0)) covers

/-- **The counterfactual of an act is the course it generates.** A balanced flow with the given
formations present, in which the part acts as in the course and the rest follows the law,
starting at the course's levels, has the course's levels and exchange. -/
theorem counterfactual_is_course (na : law.Nonanticipating) (naAct : act.Nonanticipating)
    {present : Nat → List F} {start : C → Nat} (balances : L.Balances W)
    (onPresent : ∀ t, L.present t = present t)
    (acts : L.ActsAlike ((law.withAct part act).course present start) part)
    (follows : L.Follows law part W.level) (begins : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = start c)
    (feasible : (law.withAct part act).Feasible present start) :
    (∀ t c, W.level t c = (law.withAct part act).levels present start t c) ∧
      ∀ t h c, L.take t h c = ((law.withAct part act).course present start).take t h c ∧
        L.give t h c = ((law.withAct part act).course present start).give t h c :=
  counterfactual_fixed (W' := W.withLevel ((law.withAct part act).levels present start)) na
    balances
    ((ExchangeLaw.course_balances_iff (ExchangeLaw.withAct_nonanticipating na naAct) W).mpr feasible)
    onPresent acts follows (ExchangeLaw.withAct_follows present start) begins

/-- **A law of what has stood fixes its course.** A balanced flow with the given formations
present, in which every formation exchanges as the law gives under the flow's own levels, starting
at the given levels, has the levels and exchange of the course the law generates, where that
course is feasible. The part may act by a law that reads the levels: the law here is the whole
exchange. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.course_unique {law : ExchangeLaw F C} (na : law.Nonanticipating)
    {L : Flow F C} {W : Web F C} {present : Nat → List F} {start : C → Nat}
    (balances : L.Balances W) (onPresent : ∀ t, L.present t = present t)
    (follows : L.Follows law (fun _ => false) W.level) (begins : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = start c)
    (feasible : law.Feasible present start) :
    (∀ t c, W.level t c = law.levels present start t c) ∧
      ∀ t h c, L.take t h c = (law.course present start).take t h c ∧
        L.give t h c = (law.course present start).give t h c :=
  counterfactual_fixed (W' := W.withLevel (law.levels present start)) (part := fun _ => false) na
    balances ((ExchangeLaw.course_balances_iff na W).mpr feasible) onPresent
    (fun _ no => nomatch no) follows (ExchangeLaw.course_follows _) begins

end Comparison

/-! ## The act as the input of a standard causal model -/

section Inputs

/-- The part's acts made of a sequence of moves: a law that reads nothing. A move says what each
formation takes from and gives to each condition across one step. -/
def ExchangeLaw.ofMoves (u : Nat → F → C → Nat × Nat) : ExchangeLaw F C :=
  ⟨fun t h _ c => (u t h c).1, fun t h _ c => (u t h c).2⟩

/-- One step of the course with the part's move as the input, `x_{t+1} = F(x_t, u_t)`: the
configuration is the stage and the history so far, the rest exchanging by `law`. -/
def ExchangeLaw.stepBy (law : ExchangeLaw F C) (present : Nat → List F) (part : F → Bool)
    (x : Nat × (Nat → C → Nat)) (a : F → C → Nat × Nat) (_ : Unit) : Nat × (Nat → C → Nat) :=
  (x.1 + 1, (law.withAct part (ExchangeLaw.ofMoves fun _ => a)).extend present x.2 x.1)

/-- **The act is the input of a standard causal model.** The history generated where the part
makes the moves `u` and the rest exchanges by `law` is the trajectory of the standard model whose
input is the part's move (`Becoming.Mechanism`, P68), with no noise. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.course_trajectory (law : ExchangeLaw F C) (present : Nat → List F)
    (part : F → Bool) (start : C → Nat) (u : Nat → F → C → Nat × Nat) :
    ∀ n, trajectory (law.stepBy present part) (0, fun _ => start) u (fun _ => ()) n =
      (n, (law.withAct part (ExchangeLaw.ofMoves u)).history present start n)
  | 0 => rfl
  | n + 1 => congrArg (fun x => law.stepBy present part x (u n) ())
      (ExchangeLaw.course_trajectory law present part start u n)

/-- **An act not taken is an intervention.** Where a run of the standard model of the course has
not taken the part's move `a` at `x` before `t`, the record up to `t` fixes no outcome of that move
there, while the model fixes it: P53's and P68's laws, read of acts. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.act_not_taken {law : ExchangeLaw F C} {present : Nat → List F}
    {part : F → Bool} (r : Run (inputs (law.stepBy present part))) {t : Nat}
    {x : Nat × (Nat → C → Nat)} {a : F → C → Nat × Nat} (untaken : ¬ r.Takes t x (a, ())) :
    (¬ ∃ w, r.Fixes t x (a, ()) w) ∧
      (inputs (law.stepBy present part)).step x (a, ()) (law.stepBy present part x a ()) :=
  ⟨inputs_intervention_unfixed r untaken (y := (0, fun _ _ => 0)) (z := (1, fun _ _ => 0))
      fun same => Nat.zero_ne_one (congrArg Prod.fst same),
   (inputs_model_fixes _ x a ()).1⟩

end Inputs

/-! ## The counterfactual as an unfolding (P80b) -/

section Unfolding

/-- **The course's web**: `W` with the levels the law generates. The course a law of exchange
generates is its flow (`ExchangeLaw.course`) with this web; its worlds read the two, with one kind
of relatum (`ExchangeLaw.world`) or with formations and conditions as roles of one relatum
(`ExchangeLaw.courseWorld`, `Becoming.Runs`). -/
def ExchangeLaw.courseWeb {F C : Type} (law : ExchangeLaw F C) (present : Nat → List F)
    (start : C → Nat) (W : Web F C) : Web F C :=
  W.withLevel (law.levels present start)

/-- The course a law generates over one kind of relatum, read as a world (`Becoming.Space`): its
relata exchange as the law gives, at the levels the law generates (`World.ofKind` of the course's
flow and web). -/
def ExchangeLaw.world {R : Type} (law : ExchangeLaw R R) (present : Nat → List R)
    (start : R → Nat) (W : Web R R) : World :=
  World.ofKind (law.course present start) (law.courseWeb present start W)

/-- **The counterfactual is a world.** Under a law of what has stood, the world of the course
balances exactly where the course is feasible. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.world_balances_iff {R : Type} {law : ExchangeLaw R R}
    (na : law.Nonanticipating) (present : Nat → List R) (start : R → Nat) (W : Web R R) :
    (law.world present start W).flow.Balances (law.world present start W).web ↔
      law.Feasible present start :=
  ExchangeLaw.course_balances_iff (present := present) (start := start) na W

/-- **It runs by its relata's exchanges.** Under a law of what has stood, the levels of the course
run by the law of exchange (`Becoming.Unfolding`), its moves the relata's exchanges, exactly where
the course is feasible. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.world_exchange_iff {R : Type} {law : ExchangeLaw R R}
    (na : law.Nonanticipating) (present : Nat → List R) (start : R → Nat) (W : Web R R) :
    (∀ t, (exchange R).step (law.levels present start t) ((law.world present start W).move t)
        (law.levels present start (t + 1))) ↔
      law.Feasible present start :=
  (law.world present start W).balances_iff_exchange.symm.trans
    (ExchangeLaw.world_balances_iff na present start W)

/-- The course, where feasible, as a run of the law of exchange. -/
def ExchangeLaw.worldRun {R : Type} {law : ExchangeLaw R R} (na : law.Nonanticipating)
    {present : Nat → List R} {start : R → Nat} (W : Web R R)
    (feasible : law.Feasible present start) : Run (exchange R) :=
  (law.world present start W).exchangeRun
    ((ExchangeLaw.world_balances_iff na present start W).mpr feasible)

/-- **What a determined law adds to a history is exactly the moves the history never takes.** A
determined law steps from `s` by `a` beyond what a run of it shows (`Shown`) exactly where it
steps there at all and the run never takes `a` at `s`. -/
theorem Run.beyond_shown_iff {S A : Type} {P : Process S A} (determined : Determined P)
    (r : Run P) {s : S} {a : A} :
    (∃ t, P.step s a t ∧ ¬ (Shown r.state r.act).step s a t) ↔
      (∃ t, P.step s a t) ∧ ¬ ∃ n, r.state n = s ∧ r.act n = a :=
  ⟨fun ⟨t, step, beyond⟩ =>
    ⟨⟨t, step⟩, fun ⟨n, at_s, by_a⟩ =>
      have there : P.step s a (r.state (n + 1)) := at_s ▸ by_a ▸ r.step n
      beyond ⟨n, at_s, by_a, (determined s a t (r.state (n + 1)) step there).symm⟩⟩,
   fun ⟨⟨t, step⟩, untaken⟩ =>
    ⟨t, step, fun ⟨n, at_s, by_a, _⟩ => untaken ⟨n, at_s, by_a⟩⟩⟩

/-- **The counterfactuals of a course are what its law adds to its history.** For a run of the
standard model of the course (the course an act generates is one, `ExchangeLaw.course_trajectory`),
the history shows a determined law (`Consistent`, `exists_law_iff`), and the part's move `a` at
`x` has a step of the model beyond what the history shows exactly where the history never takes
`a` at `x`. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.counterfactual_beyond_shown {law : ExchangeLaw F C} {present : Nat → List F}
    {part : F → Bool} (r : Run (inputs (law.stepBy present part)))
    (x : Nat × (Nat → C → Nat)) (a : F → C → Nat × Nat) :
    Consistent r.state r.act ∧
      ((∃ t, (inputs (law.stepBy present part)).step x (a, ()) t ∧
          ¬ (Shown r.state r.act).step x (a, ()) t) ↔
        ¬ ∃ n, r.state n = x ∧ r.act n = (a, ())) :=
  ⟨exists_law_iff.mp ⟨_, inputs_determined _, r.step⟩,
   (Run.beyond_shown_iff (inputs_determined _) r).trans
     ⟨fun both => both.2, fun untaken => ⟨⟨_, rfl⟩, untaken⟩⟩⟩

/-- The part's move formed within the unfolding by a law of the part: what `act` takes and gives
at the configuration, the stage and the history so far. -/
def ExchangeLaw.policy (act : ExchangeLaw F C) (x : Nat × (Nat → C → Nat)) :
    (F → C → Nat × Nat) × Unit :=
  (fun h c => (act.take x.1 h x.2 c, act.give x.1 h x.2 c), ())

/-- **With the act formed within, the course is the run P69's `closedBy` fixes.** A run of the
standard model of the course whose move is formed by `act` from the configuration, starting at
the starting levels, is at every step the course `law.withAct part act` generates. So two such
runs agree (`closedBy_runs_agree`): with the act formed within, the part acting otherwise is a
law of the part otherwise. -/
theorem ExchangeLaw.closedBy_course {law act : ExchangeLaw F C} {present : Nat → List F}
    {part : F → Bool} {start : C → Nat}
    (r : Run (closedBy (inputs (law.stepBy present part)) act.policy))
    (begins : r.state 0 = ((0, fun _ => start), act.policy (0, fun _ => start))) :
    ∀ n, (r.state n).1 = (n, (law.withAct part act).history present start n)
  | 0 => congrArg Prod.fst begins
  | n + 1 =>
    have formed : (r.state n).2 = act.policy (r.state n).1 :=
      match n with
      | 0 => (congrArg Prod.snd begins).trans (congrArg act.policy (congrArg Prod.fst begins)).symm
      | k + 1 => (r.step k).2
    (r.step n).1.trans
      ((congrArg (fun m => law.stepBy present part (r.state n).1 m.1 m.2) formed).trans
        (congrArg (fun x => law.stepBy present part x (act.policy x).1 ())
          (ExchangeLaw.closedBy_course r begins n)))

end Unfolding

/-! ## Witness: the loop as courses of one law -/

namespace Loop

/-- The second's law: across the step after its own condition fell, it takes two units of the
first's condition; nothing else. -/
def replyLaw : ExchangeLaw Formation Place :=
  ⟨fun t h l c => match h, c with
    | .first, .ofFirst => 0
    | .first, .ofSecond => 0
    | .second, .ofFirst => if l t .ofSecond < l (t - 1) .ofSecond then 2 else 0
    | .second, .ofSecond => 0,
   fun _ _ _ _ => 0⟩

/-- The second's law reads what has stood. -/
theorem replyLaw_nonanticipating : replyLaw.Nonanticipating
  | t, .second, l, l', agree, .ofFirst =>
    ⟨(congrArg (fun a => if a < l (t - 1) .ofSecond then 2 else 0)
        (agree t (Nat.le_refl t) .ofSecond)).trans
      (congrArg (fun b => if l' t .ofSecond < b then 2 else 0)
        (agree (t - 1) (Nat.sub_le t 1) .ofSecond)), rfl⟩
  | _, .second, _, _, _, .ofSecond => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | _, .first, _, _, _, .ofFirst => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | _, .first, _, _, _, .ofSecond => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩

/-- The second's law reads only what the second observes, its own condition. -/
theorem replyLaw_through : replyLaw.Through web paired firstPart
  | .second, _, t, l, l', alike, .ofFirst =>
    ⟨(congrArg (fun a => if a < l (t - 1) .ofSecond then 2 else 0)
        ((alike t (Nat.le_refl t)).2 .ofSecond trivial)).trans
      (congrArg (fun b => if l' t .ofSecond < b then 2 else 0)
        ((alike (t - 1) (Nat.sub_le t 1)).2 .ofSecond trivial)), rfl⟩
  | .second, _, _, _, _, _, .ofSecond => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .first, no, _, _, _, _, _ => nomatch no

/-- With the act, the second follows its law. -/
theorem escalation_follows : escalation.Follows replyLaw firstPart web.level
  | .second, _, 0, .ofFirst => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .second, _, 1, .ofFirst => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .second, _, _ + 2, .ofFirst => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .second, _, _, .ofSecond => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .first, no, _, _ => nomatch no

/-- Without the act, the second follows the same law. -/
theorem quiet_follows : quiet.Follows replyLaw firstPart quietWeb.level
  | .second, _, _, .ofFirst => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .second, _, _, .ofSecond => ⟨rfl, rfl⟩
  | .first, no, _, _ => nomatch no

/-- P65's `Loop.responds`, checked there case by case, follows from the second's law. -/
theorem responds_by_law : escalation.RespondsThroughCoupling quiet web quietWeb paired firstPart :=
  replyLaw_through.responds escalation_follows quiet_follows

/-- **Each course is fixed by the first's act.** A balanced flow with both formations present,
the second following its law, starting at two units on each condition, has the escalation's levels
and exchange where the first acts as there, and the quiet flow's where the first does not act. -/
theorem courses_fixed {L : Flow Formation Place} {W : Web Formation Place}
    (balanced : L.Balances W) (present : ∀ t, L.present t = members)
    (follows : L.Follows replyLaw firstPart W.level) (start : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = 2) :
    (L.ActsAlike escalation firstPart →
      (∀ t c, W.level t c = web.level t c) ∧
        ∀ t h c, L.take t h c = escalation.take t h c ∧ L.give t h c = escalation.give t h c) ∧
    (L.ActsAlike quiet firstPart →
      (∀ t c, W.level t c = quietWeb.level t c) ∧
        ∀ t h c, L.take t h c = quiet.take t h c ∧ L.give t h c = quiet.give t h c) :=
  have begins : ∀ c, W.level 0 c = web.level 0 c := fun c =>
    (start c).trans (match c with
      | .ofFirst => rfl
      | .ofSecond => rfl)
  ⟨fun acts => counterfactual_fixed replyLaw_nonanticipating balanced Loop.balances present acts
      follows escalation_follows begins,
   fun acts => counterfactual_fixed replyLaw_nonanticipating balanced Loop.quiet_balances present
      acts follows quiet_follows fun c => (begins c).trans (match c with
        | .ofFirst => rfl
        | .ofSecond => rfl)⟩

end Loop

end Becoming
